1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,761
Orchard Core stores most of its data as JSON documents, and an index

2
00:00:05,761 --> 00:00:07,948
is what lets you query them.

3
00:00:07,948 --> 00:00:12,967
In this video, you define a YesSql index, register it, create its table,

4
00:00:12,967 --> 00:00:15,090
and query it with ISession.

5
00:00:19,690 --> 00:00:24,490
Orchard Core does not use an ORM such as Entity Framework.

6
00:00:24,490 --> 00:00:31,540
Content items, users, settings, and workflows are saved with YesSql, a document database

7
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:34,765
interface over a relational database:

8
00:00:34,765 --> 00:00:38,290
SQL Server, SQLite, MySQL, or PostgreSQL.

9
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:44,380
YesSql serializes an object to JSON and stores it as a row of a document table.

10
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:48,401
There is no schema to migrate, so adding a part or a field

11
00:00:48,401 --> 00:00:52,170
to a content type never requires a database migration.

12
00:00:52,920 --> 00:00:57,360
The limit is that SQL cannot query inside a JSON document efficiently.

13
00:00:57,360 --> 00:00:58,821
An index solves it:

14
00:00:58,821 --> 00:01:03,086
a plain class with the properties you want to query, kept by YesSql

15
00:01:03,086 --> 00:01:04,780
in a regular SQL table.

16
00:01:05,530 --> 00:01:11,230
When a document is created, updated, or deleted, YesSql recomputes its index rows

17
00:01:11,230 --> 00:01:13,195
in the same transaction.

18
00:01:13,195 --> 00:01:16,602
A property you query must be part of an index.

19
00:01:16,602 --> 00:01:19,550
Everything else stays only in the JSON.

20
00:01:23,750 --> 00:01:27,254
A map index is a class that inherits from MapIndex.

21
00:01:27,254 --> 00:01:30,329
Its properties are the columns you query on.

22
00:01:30,329 --> 00:01:35,370
ProductIndex has a Sku, a Price, and the Published flag of the content item.

23
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:40,239
An IndexProvider describes how to map a document type to index rows.

24
00:01:40,239 --> 00:01:44,693
In Describe, For names the index, When selects the content items that have

25
00:01:44,693 --> 00:01:47,810
a ProductPart, and Map builds a row from the part.

26
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:53,189
Each version of a content item is its own document, so ProductIndex has

27
00:01:53,189 --> 00:01:54,992
a row for every version.

28
00:01:54,992 --> 00:01:59,020
The Published flag lets a query choose the published version.

29
00:01:59,770 --> 00:02:03,664
Map can return null, and the document then has no row.

30
00:02:03,664 --> 00:02:07,233
AliasPartIndex does this when the alias is empty.

31
00:02:07,233 --> 00:02:12,750
It is a map index too, with rows only for content items that have an AliasPart.

32
00:02:16,950 --> 00:02:22,801
In the Startup class of your module, AddIndexProvider registers the provider as a singleton.

33
00:02:22,801 --> 00:02:31,040
A provider that needs scoped services implements IScopedIndexProvider, and AddScopedIndexProvider registers it at the session level.

34
00:02:31,790 --> 00:02:35,024
The index table comes from a data migration.

35
00:02:35,024 --> 00:02:39,357
CreateMapIndexTableAsync declares a column for each property.

36
00:02:39,357 --> 00:02:45,372
The documentation on data migrations advises setting the length of every string column,

37
00:02:45,372 --> 00:02:46,860
as Sku does here.

38
00:02:47,610 --> 00:02:53,714
AlterIndexTableAsync with CreateIndex adds a database index on DocumentId and the columns you

39
00:02:53,714 --> 00:02:56,612
filter on, which speeds up those queries.

40
00:02:56,612 --> 00:03:00,374
Orchard Core runs the migration when the tenant starts.

41
00:03:00,374 --> 00:03:03,210
A separate video covers data migrations.

42
00:03:07,410 --> 00:03:08,690
Inject ISession and call Query with the document type and the index type.

43
00:03:08,690 --> 00:03:09,970
The predicate filters the index table with SQL, and YesSql then loads and

44
00:03:09,970 --> 00:03:10,650
deserializes the matching documents.

45
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:13,608
The session is a unit of work:

46
00:03:13,608 --> 00:03:19,678
SaveAsync and Delete are buffered, and SaveChangesAsync runs the commands in one transaction.

47
00:03:19,678 --> 00:03:23,848
Orchard Core calls it when the shell scope of the tenant ends.

48
00:03:23,848 --> 00:03:27,650
A session from any other scope is not committed for you.

49
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:31,548
To combine indexes, call With on the query.

50
00:03:31,548 --> 00:03:39,130
Orchard Core's user picker filters users through UserByRoleNameIndex and UserIndex, and takes 50 of the matches.

51
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:45,611
For content items, prefer IContentManager, which handles loading, versions, and handlers.

52
00:03:45,611 --> 00:03:49,170
Use ISession when you need to query your own indexes.

53
00:03:53,370 --> 00:03:58,195
A reduce index aggregates a group of documents, like a SQL GROUP BY.

54
00:03:58,195 --> 00:04:01,782
UserByRoleNameIndex holds a RoleName and a Count.

55
00:04:01,782 --> 00:04:05,955
Map emits a row for each role of a user, and Group chooses

56
00:04:05,955 --> 00:04:07,650
RoleName as the key.

57
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:11,838
Reduce adds the counts of a group into one row.

58
00:04:11,838 --> 00:04:17,677
Delete subtracts the counts of the removed rows, and returns null when none is left.

59
00:04:17,677 --> 00:04:21,050
CreateReduceIndexTableAsync creates its table.

60
00:04:25,301 --> 00:04:29,674
The SQL Queries feature adds Run SQL Query, under Search, Queries.

61
00:04:29,674 --> 00:04:33,925
It runs SQL against the database of the site, and adds the table

62
00:04:33,925 --> 00:04:36,051
prefix of the tenant for you.

63
00:04:36,538 --> 00:04:39,863
A link on this page queries all content items.

64
00:04:39,863 --> 00:04:45,169
It selects every row of ContentItemIndex, the table that YesSql keeps in step

65
00:04:45,169 --> 00:04:47,598
with the content item documents.

66
00:04:48,074 --> 00:04:50,547
Each result is a row of the index.

67
00:04:50,547 --> 00:04:56,112
It holds the columns of ContentItemIndex, including a DocumentId that points back to

68
00:04:56,112 --> 00:04:58,894
the document the row was computed from.

69
00:04:59,384 --> 00:05:02,879
A reduce index row does not point to one document:

70
00:05:02,879 --> 00:05:05,125
it aggregates a group of them.

71
00:05:05,125 --> 00:05:10,804
In UserByRoleNameIndex, each row is a role, with the Count of the users that have it.

72
00:05:15,004 --> 00:05:18,326
A map index can hold several records for a document.

73
00:05:18,326 --> 00:05:22,450
A query that joins it must then group its matches by document, and

74
00:05:22,450 --> 00:05:25,944
a page has to read every match before it can be sorted.

75
00:05:26,694 --> 00:05:32,579
SingleRecordPerDocument declares that the map returns at most one record per document.

76
00:05:32,579 --> 00:05:37,120
A query that joins only such indexes is not grouped, and saving a

77
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:40,254
document throws if the map returns several.

78
00:05:41,004 --> 00:05:46,835
ExclusiveToDocumentType declares that only documents of the provider's type have records in the

79
00:05:46,835 --> 00:05:51,568
index, so a count reads the index tables without joining the document table.

80
00:05:51,568 --> 00:05:53,704
ContentItemIndex declares both.

81
00:05:54,454 --> 00:05:59,843
NoIndexOptimizations opts a query out of these optimizations, as Orchard Core does for

82
00:05:59,843 --> 00:06:02,303
a text search in the content picker.

83
00:06:02,303 --> 00:06:07,633
SkipDeduplication skips the grouping when a query joins an index with several records

84
00:06:07,633 --> 00:06:09,214
but matches only one.

85
00:06:11,214 --> 00:06:13,064
That's a YesSql index:

86
00:06:13,064 --> 00:06:17,357
a class, a provider, a table, and a query through ISession.

87
00:06:17,357 --> 00:06:22,047
The documentation page, How YesSql works, goes through each step.

88
00:06:22,047 --> 00:06:23,764
Thanks for watching.

